::ALG2_U02_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If x represents a side length, which solution should be rejected? {
~x = 2 #Positive lengths may be viable.
=x = -6 #Correct. A side length cannot be negative.
~x = 5 #Positive lengths may be viable.
~x = 8 #Positive lengths may be viable.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does a zero of a height model usually represent? {
~maximum height #Maximum height is at the vertex.
~initial velocity #Velocity is not shown by a zero alone.
~axis of symmetry #Axis of symmetry gives vertex time.
=a time when height is 0 #Correct. A zero means output height is 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does the vertex of a downward-opening height model represent? {
=maximum height #Correct. Downward-opening height models have a maximum at the vertex.
~landing time only #Landing time is a zero.
~starting time only #Starting time is often t = 0.
~a rejected value #The vertex is usually meaningful.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What should a final modeling answer include? {
~only the equation #The equation alone is incomplete.
~only a graph label #A graph label alone is incomplete.
=the solution and what it means in context #Correct. A modeling solution must interpret the answer.
~only the units #Units matter but are not enough.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why can an algebraic solution be non-viable? {
=it may violate the context or constraints #Correct. Context determines viability. 
~it is always complex #Not all non-viable values are complex.
~it has a variable #Variables are part of models.
~it came from factoring #Factoring is a valid method.
}

